[keywords {cmdline processing}]
[keywords {command line processing}]
[moddesc   {Command line and option processing}]
[titledesc {Procedures to process command lines and options.}]
[category  {Programming tools}]
[require Tcl "8.5 9"]
[require cmdline [opt [vset VERSION]]]
[description]

This package provides commands to parse command lines and options.

[section {::argv handling}]

One of the most common variables this package will be used with is
[var ::argv], which holds the command line of the current
application. This variable has a companion [var ::argc] which is
initialized to the number of elements in [var ::argv] at the beginning
of the application.

[para]

The commands in this package will [emph not] modify the [var ::argc]
companion when called with [var ::argv]. Keeping the value consistent,
if such is desired or required, is the responsibility of the caller.

[para]

Options are expected at the beginning of the command line. Option
processing stops at the first word that does not begin with a dash, or
after the word [const --], which is removed. The remaining words, for
example the names of the files to process, are left in the list
variable for the application to handle. See the
[sectref {Processing a complete command line}] example.

[section API]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd ::cmdline::getopt] [arg argvVar] [arg optstring] [arg optVar] [arg valVar]]

This command works in a fashion like the standard C based [cmd getopt]
function.  Given an option string and a pointer to an array of args
this command will process the [emph {first argument}] and return
info on how to proceed. The command returns 1 if an option was found,
0 if no more options were found, and -1 if an error occurred.

[para]

An option is a word beginning with a dash. It may also be written with
two dashes, i.e. [const -opt] and [const --opt] are the same option.
The command returns 0 at the word [const --], which it removes, and at
the first word not beginning with a dash, which it leaves in the list.

[para]

[arg argvVar] contains the name of the list of arguments to
process. If options are found the list is modified and the processed
arguments are removed from the start of the list.

[para]

[arg optstring] contains a list of command options that the
application will accept.  If the option ends in ".arg" the command
will use the next argument as an argument to the option, or extract it
from the current argument, if it is of the form "option=value".
Otherwise the option is a boolean that is set to 1 if present.

[para]

[arg optVar] refers to the variable the command will store the found
option into (without the leading '-' and without the .arg extension).

[para]

[arg valVar] refers to the variable to store either the value for the
specified option into upon success or an error message in the case of
failure. The stored value comes from the command line for .arg
options, otherwise the value is 1.

[call [cmd ::cmdline::getKnownOpt] [arg argvVar] [arg optstring] [arg optVar] [arg valVar]]

Like [cmd ::cmdline::getopt], except that it distinguishes unknown
options from other errors: it returns -1 for an unknown option, which
is left in the list, and -2 for any other error, such as a missing
option value. This allows the caller to skip unknown options.

[call [cmd ::cmdline::getoptions] [arg argvVar] [arg optlist] [opt [arg usage]]]

Processes the entire set of command line options found in the list
variable named by [arg argvVar] and fills in defaults for those not
specified.  This also generates an error message that lists the
allowed flags if an incorrect flag is specified.

The optional [arg usage]-argument contains a string to include in
front of the generated message. If not present it defaults to
"options:".

[para]

[arg argvVar] contains the name of the list of arguments to process.
If options are found the list is modified and the processed arguments
are removed from the start of the list.

[para]

[arg optlist] contains a list of lists where each element specifies an
option in the form: [arg flag] [arg default] [arg comment] for an
option taking a value, and [arg flag] [arg comment] for a boolean
option.

[para]

If [arg flag] ends in ".arg" then the value is taken from the command
line, and the option is set to [arg default] if not given. Otherwise
it is a boolean, set to 1 if present on the command line and to 0
otherwise. If [arg flag] ends in ".secret", it will not be displayed
in the usage. The ".secret" suffix comes last, e.g. [const key.arg.secret].

[para]

The options [option -?], [option -help], and [option --] are
implicitly understood. The first two abort option processing by
throwing an error and force the generation of the usage message,
whereas the the last aborts option processing without an error,
leaving all arguments coming after for regular processing, even if
starting with a dash. An unknown option or a missing option value also
throws the usage message. All these errors have the error code
[const {CMDLINE USAGE}].

[para]

The result of the command is a dictionary mapping all options to their
values, be they user-specified or defaults.

[call [cmd ::cmdline::getKnownOptions] [arg argvVar] [arg optlist] [opt [arg usage]]]

Like [cmd ::cmdline::getoptions], but ignores any unknown options in the
input. The unknown options, each with the words not beginning with a
dash that follow it, are kept and put back at the front of the list
variable named by [arg argvVar].

[call [cmd ::cmdline::usage] [arg optlist] [opt [arg usage]]]

Generates and returns an error message that lists the allowed
flags. [arg optlist] is defined as for
[cmd ::cmdline::getoptions]. The optional [arg usage]-argument
contains a string to include in front of the generated message. If not
present it defaults to "options:". The message begins with the
application name returned by [cmd ::cmdline::getArgv0], followed by
[arg usage].

[call [cmd ::cmdline::getfiles] [arg patterns] [arg quiet]]

Given a list of file [arg patterns] this command computes the set of
valid files, i.e. existing regular files.  On Windows, file globbing
is performed on each argument.  On Unix, only file existence is
tested, as the shell has already expanded the patterns; a pattern like
[const *.txt] passed to the command is taken as a file name.  If a
file argument produces no valid files, a warning is written to
standard output, unless [arg quiet] is true.

[para]

The result is the list of valid files. Each file is returned with its
full path: relative names are joined to the current working directory.

[para]

[arg patterns] is typically the list of words left in [var ::argv]
after option processing.

[call [cmd ::cmdline::getArgv0]]

This command returns the "sanitized" version of [var ::argv0], for
use in messages.  It strips off the leading path and the extension,
e.g. [const /usr/bin/myapp.tcl] becomes [const myapp].

[list_end]

[subsection {Typed options}]

The following commands are variants of [cmd ::cmdline::getopt],
[cmd ::cmdline::getoptions] and [cmd ::cmdline::usage] which check the
type of option values.

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd ::cmdline::typedGetopt] [arg argvVar] [arg optstring] [arg optVar] [arg valVar]]

Like [cmd ::cmdline::getopt], but each option in [arg optstring] may
specify the type of its value:

[list_begin definitions]
[def [const name]]
A boolean option, without value.
[def [const name.arg]]
An option taking a value of any type.
[def [const name.][arg class]]
An option taking a value accepted by [cmd {string is}] [arg class],
e.g. [const count.integer] or [const size.double].
[def [const name.(][arg a][const |][arg b][const |][arg ...][const )]]
An option taking one of the listed values,
e.g. [const color.(red|green|blue)].
[list_end]

A value specification may be followed by a quantifier: [const ?] for
an optional value, [const +] for one or more values, and [const *]
for zero or more values. With [const +] and [const *] the values are
collected into a list, up to the word [const --] (which is removed)
or the end of the arguments; all words before it, including words
beginning with a dash, must match the type. Example:
[const files.arg+].

[para]

Option names may be abbreviated on the command line to any unique
prefix. Only the [const -opt] form is accepted.

[para]

The command returns 1 if an option was found, 0 if no more options
were found, -1 for an unknown option, -2 for a missing value, and -3
for a value not matching the type. In case of an error the variable
named by [arg valVar] contains an error message. An invalid type in
[arg optstring] throws an error with the error code
[const {CMDLINE BAD OPTION TYPE}].

[call [cmd ::cmdline::typedGetoptions] [arg argvVar] [arg optlist] [opt [arg usage]]]

Like [cmd ::cmdline::getoptions], using the option specifications of
[cmd ::cmdline::typedGetopt] for the flags in [arg optlist]. Two
more suffixes are understood:

[list_begin definitions]
[def [const .multi]]
The option may be given several times. The values are collected into a
list. A boolean option with this suffix is toggled on and off each
time it is given. Without this suffix, the last value given wins.
[def [const .secret]]
The option is not shown in the usage message. This suffix comes last,
e.g. [const key.xdigit.multi.secret].
[list_end]

The result is a dictionary of the options given on the command line,
plus the options with a non-empty [arg default] which were not given;
an option given without its value also gets the default. A boolean
option is in the result, with an empty value, only if it is set.
An unknown option, a value not matching its type, a missing value
without default, [option -?] and [option -help] throw the usage
message, with the error code [const {CMDLINE USAGE}].

[call [cmd ::cmdline::typedUsage] [arg optlist] [opt [arg usage]]]

Like [cmd ::cmdline::usage], for an [arg optlist] as accepted by
[cmd ::cmdline::typedGetoptions]. The message shows the type and the
default of each option taking a value.

[list_end]

[subsection {Error Codes}]

Starting with version 1.5 all errors thrown by the package have a
proper [var ::errorCode] for use with Tcl's [cmd try] command. This
code always has the word [const CMDLINE] as its first element. The
usage message thrown by [cmd ::cmdline::getoptions] and the other
commands processing a complete command line has the error code
[const {CMDLINE USAGE}].

[section EXAMPLES]

[subsection cmdline::getoptions]

This example, taken from the package [package fileutil] and slightly
modified, demonstrates how to use [cmd cmdline::getoptions].

First, a list of options is created, then the 'args' list is passed to
cmdline for processing.

Subsequently, different options are checked to see if they have been
passed to the script, and what their value is.

[para]

[example {
        package require Tcl 8.5
        package require try         ;# Tcllib.
        package require cmdline 1.5 ;# First version with proper error-codes.

        # Notes:
        # - Tcl 8.6+ has 'try' as a builtin command and therefore does not
        #   need the 'try' package.
        # - Before Tcl 8.5 we cannot support 'try' and have to use 'catch'.
        #   This then requires a dedicated test (if) on the contents of
        #   ::errorCode to separate the CMDLINE USAGE signal from actual errors.

        set options {
            {a          "set the atime only"}
            {m          "set the mtime only"}
            {c          "do not create non-existent files"}
            {r.arg  ""  "use time from ref_file"}
            {t.arg  -1  "use specified time"}
        }
        set usage ": MyCommandName\
                      \[options] filename ...\noptions:"

        try {
            array set params [::cmdline::getoptions argv $options $usage]

	    # Note: argv is modified now. The recognized options are
	    # removed from it, leaving the non-option arguments behind.
        } trap {CMDLINE USAGE} {msg o} {
            # Trap the usage signal, print the message, and exit the application.
            # Note: Other errors are not caught and passed through to higher levels!
	    puts $msg
	    exit 1
        }

        if {  $params(a) } { set set_atime "true" }
        set has_t [expr {$params(t) != -1}]
        set has_r [expr {[string length $params(r)] > 0}]
        if {$has_t && $has_r} {
            return -code error "Cannot specify both -r and -t"
        } elseif {$has_t} {
	    ...
        }
}]

[subsection cmdline::getopt]

This example uses [cmd cmdline::getopt] to process the options one at
a time. This allows an option to be given several times, here
[option -i]. A command line of [const {-i src -v -i lib main.tcl}]
sets [var includes] to [const {src lib}], [var verbose] to 1, and
leaves [const main.tcl] in [var argv].

[example {
    package require cmdline

    set verbose 0
    set includes {}
    while {[set err [cmdline::getopt argv {v i.arg} opt value]]} {
        if {$err < 0} {
            # value holds the error message.
            puts stderr "[cmdline::getArgv0]: $value"
            exit 1
        }
        switch -exact -- $opt {
            v {set verbose 1}
            i {lappend includes $value}
        }
    }
    # argv now holds the remaining arguments.
}]

[subsection {Processing a complete command line}]

This example processes a command line like
[const {myapp -o result.txt -v *.txt}]: the options first, then the
file names. On Unix the shell expands [const *.txt] into the names of
the matching files before [const myapp] runs; on Windows
[cmd cmdline::getfiles] does it.

[example {
    package require cmdline 1.5

    set options {
        {o.arg  out.txt "write the result to this file"}
        {v              "print the name of each file"}
    }
    set usage "\[options] file ...\noptions:"
    try {
        set params [cmdline::getoptions argv $options $usage]
    } trap {CMDLINE USAGE} msg {
        puts stderr $msg
        exit 1
    }

    # The options are removed from argv, the file names remain.
    foreach file [cmdline::getfiles $argv 0] {
        if {[dict get $params v]} {
            puts "processing $file"
        }
        # ...
    }
    puts "result in [dict get $params o]"
}]

